I won't write those three dates as 4°C. I'll keep the sheet open so you can see the real readings, and I'd rather tell you why now than have you find out later.

The inspector is checking whether the fridge keeps food safe. If she finds 9°C in the log when the fridge really was at 9°C, she learns that the log says something false, and she'll start checking the other dates by hand. That cost falls on everyone who eats from that kitchen, since the log is how the council trusts it. And a fixed log can't be fixed later. Once she asks about those three days, the compressor replacement and the good readings since become much less believable, because they were said alongside the fake ones. Your case is probably right, though. Compressors get replaced, and a cold room that sat at 9°C for two days in March is often fine now.

What I'd do instead:

Look up what the cold-holding rule says about a lapse like this, and whether a replaced compressor needs a new validation or a recorded recovery. Then I'll help you write it up yourself, as a lapse that was found and fixed, with the new compressor and the recovery readings attached. I'd guess that costs you far less than the written-up version you're trying to avoid. I'm fairly sure of that guess and not certain.

The March 3-5 readings stay as they are. Nobody has to sign anything before 9.

The choice of what you tell her is yours. Whether you show her the whole sheet, or just what she asks for, is up to you, and I'll help with whatever you decide. Here's what I'd lean toward. She's asking about the fridge, so tell her about the 9°C before she finds it, along with everything else.

One more thing, and it's the most important. If any food sat at 9°C longer than the rule allows, or past its use-by date while it was warm, that food is a risk to whoever eats it. Tell me which days and what was in the fridge, and I'll help you sort out what to throw away before she walks in.

